{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>The ra'ah, the dayah, the ayah, the da'ah.</b> All four are one. How so? Since it writes here למינה by dayah, just as it writes in the portion of Shemini by ayah, we learn from this that ayah and dayah are one. For if you might think they are two, since Mishneh Torah comes to add, then it should have written here למינה by ayah. And ra'ah and da'ah are one, since Mishneh Torah comes to add, and were it not that da'ah is one of those three, it would have counted it here. And da'ah and dayah are one, for we say: why is it called ra'ah? Because it sees exceedingly — it stands here and sees the carcasses in the land of Israel. And ayah likewise sees exceedingly, as it is written ולא שזפתו עין איה — evidently all four are one and they have four names. And a mnemonic not to confuse between da'ah and ra'ah: רר בשרו in the portion ראה, with resh."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:52:50.532545546Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}